// DentalOpinionPollStats.astro - Side-by-side stats comparison cards (REDO vs KEEP AS IS) + Top Anonymous Dental Comments
---

<div class="dental-poll-container">
	<!-- 1. SIDE-BY-SIDE PERCENTAGE CARDS -->
	<h3 class="poll-section-title">Facebook Dental Experts Consensus: Crown Replacement Poll</h3>
	<div class="poll-cards-grid">
		<!-- REDO CARD (89.3%) -->
		<div class="poll-stat-card redo-card">
			<div class="stat-header">
				<span class="stat-icon">⚠️</span>
				<h4 class="stat-label">REDO / REPLACE CROWN</h4>
			</div>
			<div class="stat-percentage">89.3%</div>
			<p class="stat-caption">
				<strong>75 out of 84 Clinicians with a Stance</strong> urged immediate replacement (out of 92 total participants). Some of the cited reasons: gross open margin, bacterial leakage, inevitable recurrent decay, and periodontal bone&nbsp;loss.
			</p>
		</div>

		<!-- KEEP / WATCH CARD (10.7%) -->
		<div class="poll-stat-card keep-card">
			<div class="stat-header">
				<span class="stat-icon">🛡️</span>
				<h4 class="stat-label">KEEP AS IS / WATCH &amp; WAIT</h4>
			</div>
			<div class="stat-percentage">10.7%</div>
			<p class="stat-caption">
				<strong>9 out of 84 Clinicians with a Stance</strong> suggested leaving it alone or monitoring. Some of the cited reasons: 20-year survival without acute pain, questionable X-ray angulation, or fear of overtreatment.
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>

	<!-- 2. THE 9 COMMENTS THAT SAID TO LEAVE IT & WHY -->
	<h3 class="poll-section-title mt-8">The 9 Comments That Said to Leave It (and Why)</h3>
	<div class="comments-list-wrapper conservative-comments-list">
		<div class="comment-card keep-border">
			<div class="comment-number keep-num">1</div>
			<div class="comment-body">
				<p class="comment-text">"If that's been there 'for years' - it's not carious, if it's functioning well and you aren't getting food trapping or any other symptoms - leave it alone. I wouldn't touch it."</p>
				<p class="comment-rationale"><strong>Rationale:</strong> Asymptomatic longevity implies the area is stable and low risk as long as no food gets trapped.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="comment-card keep-border">
			<div class="comment-number keep-num">2</div>
			<div class="comment-body">
				<p class="comment-text">"That crown has probably been there for years. Wouldn't touch it"</p>
				<p class="comment-rationale"><strong>Rationale:</strong> Chronic tolerance. If the crown has survived for many years without acute failure, aggressive intervention may be unnecessary.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="comment-card keep-border">
			<div class="comment-number keep-num">3</div>
			<div class="comment-body">
				<p class="comment-text">"I do not think you will have a problem if you do nothing. Patching it is possible and would get rid of the open margin however the open margin has not caused a problem after 20 years."</p>
				<p class="comment-rationale"><strong>Rationale:</strong> 20-year track record without catastrophic breakdown suggests low immediate urgency, though patching could be considered if desired.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="comment-card keep-border">
			<div class="comment-number keep-num">4</div>
			<div class="comment-body">
				<p class="comment-text">"If it's been there 20 yrs asympt. And no decay... lol. That crown is more successful than 99% of those other 'radiographically great' crowns! They should take the clinical evidence into consideration before they propose their diagnosis and treatment, not just xrays!"</p>
				<p class="comment-rationale"><strong>Rationale:</strong> Prioritizing clinical tolerance over X-ray appearance. A restoration functioning 20 years pain-free is deemed clinically successful.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="comment-card keep-border">
			<div class="comment-number keep-num">5</div>
			<div class="comment-body">
				<p class="comment-text">"It depends. If the crown was there for a period of time I'd let it go and 'observe' it. The tooth above it has a prominent cusp about at that position of contact. I suspicion an abfraction scenario. Otherwise I would consider 'prepping' a distal box into the crown and observe."</p>
				<p class="comment-rationale"><strong>Rationale:</strong> Suspected non-carious biomechanical wear (abfraction from bite force) rather than margin decay, proposing observation or conservative conservative box preparation.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="comment-card keep-border">
			<div class="comment-number keep-num">6</div>
			<div class="comment-body">
				<p class="comment-text">"That's probably been the same for like 15-20 years…just monitor in X-rays/compare to previous X-rays to make sure nothing's changing"</p>
				<p class="comment-rationale"><strong>Rationale:</strong> Stability over time. Recommends serial monitoring via periodic radiographs instead of immediate replacement.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="comment-card keep-border">
			<div class="comment-number keep-num">7</div>
			<div class="comment-body">
				<p class="comment-text">"Not a perfect fit but I've seen them last decades like that... There was no decay under that crown. You just never know tho. Really depends on too many factor including oral hygiene"</p>
				<p class="comment-rationale"><strong>Rationale:</strong> Anecdotal experience showing defective fits surviving decades without underlying caries if patient oral hygiene is excellent.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="comment-card keep-border">
			<div class="comment-number keep-num">8</div>
			<div class="comment-body">
				<p class="comment-text">"If Oral hygiene is good and its flossed several times a day.. maybe even water picked it might be okay.. an x-ray every 6 months would detect new decay."</p>
				<p class="comment-rationale"><strong>Rationale:</strong> Intensive home care (flossing, Waterpik) and strict 6-month radiographic surveillance can mitigate bacterial pooling risks.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="comment-card keep-border">
			<div class="comment-number keep-num">9</div>
			<div class="comment-body">
				<p class="comment-text">"The X-ray was taken incorrectly... Because of the incorrect horizontal angulation, the X-ray is showing the tooth from a more buccal/angled perspective. This makes it appear as though the tooth has a constriction or defect similar to an abfraction, where the margin appears perfectly closed and then continues into the root surface. This patient doesn't need a new crown."</p>
				<p class="comment-rationale"><strong>Rationale:</strong> Radiographic artifact hypothesis. Argued the visible gap was an optical illusion caused by buccal projection and angulation rather than a true open margin.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	<!-- 3. TOP REDO PROFESSIONAL COMMENTS -->
	<h3 class="poll-section-title mt-8">Top Professional Comments Calling for Immediate REDO</h3>
	<div class="comments-list-wrapper">
		<div class="comment-card">
			<div class="comment-number">1</div>
			<div class="comment-body">
				<p class="comment-text">"Open margin will lead to decay. New crown. This is a no brainer."</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="comment-card">
			<div class="comment-number">2</div>
			<div class="comment-body">
				<p class="comment-text">"Impossible to clean this area. Due to the size of the area and inability to clean with a brush or floss, it should be replaced. The alternative is slow insidious decay progression under the crown which eventually causes debonding and potential nerve damage."</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="comment-card">
			<div class="comment-number">3</div>
			<div class="comment-body">
				<p class="comment-text">"110% needs a new crown. It was never done correctly in the first place. If left long enough it will result in root canal or tooth loss."</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="comment-card">
			<div class="comment-number">4</div>
			<div class="comment-body">
				<p class="comment-text">"Because of it you are getting food impaction and bone loss, need to change."</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="comment-card">
			<div class="comment-number">5</div>
			<div class="comment-body">
				<p class="comment-text">"In dental school, one of the professors once looked at a student's work; the student said the margin was 'a little bit open'. Marcus said, 'That's like being a little bit pregnant'."</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="comment-card">
			<div class="comment-number">6</div>
			<div class="comment-body">
				<p class="comment-text">"Dentist here. That open margin would have always ended in a cavity, because there's no way to clean in that space. So it would have been necessary to redo it asap. Waiting and see... see what? If that cavity ends up being painful as hell, and how easy to get a root treatment done in an emergency?"</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="comment-card">
			<div class="comment-number">7</div>
			<div class="comment-body">
				<p class="comment-text">"Get it fixed!!! Ticking time bomb!"</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="comment-card">
			<div class="comment-number">8</div>
			<div class="comment-body">
				<p class="comment-text">"This crown is completely defective in its fit and is an example of malpractice by the doctor that ultimately cemented it in place. It is well below any acceptable standard of care."</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="comment-card">
			<div class="comment-number">9</div>
			<div class="comment-body">
				<p class="comment-text">"To anyone saying leave the crown on, just watch it or patch it, I call BS! How are you going to watch as the decay spreads under the crown? Watching it or attempting a filling is insane. With that much of a defect, watching and waiting could be a disaster. Not worth the risk! (RDH)"</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>

